WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.510
Every matrix has four fundamental subspaces

2
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.709
and four numbers say how big they are.

3
00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:12.334
Here is the drone matrix again

4
00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:15.780
four buttons, three directions, rank two.

5
00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:18.677
So far we read it by columns.

6
00:00:18.677 --> 00:00:22.367
Now read the same twelve numbers by rows.

7
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:25.968
Row one is the east step of each button

8
00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:28.077
row two the north step

9
00:00:28.077 --> 00:00:30.097
row three the up step.

10
00:00:30.097 --> 00:00:34.050
The transpose turns these rows into columns.

11
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.363
Two questions about rows

12
00:00:36.445 --> 00:00:38.326
give two new subspaces

13
00:00:38.326 --> 00:00:41.925
which combinations of the rows can we make

14
00:00:41.925 --> 00:00:45.360
and which combinations give the zero row?

15
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.239
Before computing

16
00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:50.386
is some combination of the drone's

17
00:00:50.476 --> 00:00:52.634
three rows the zero row?

18
00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:56.501
Hint: think of the plane from lecture six.

19
00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:58.030
Pause and think.

20
00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.427
The row space is all combinations

21
00:01:04.512 --> 00:01:05.536
of the rows.

22
00:01:05.536 --> 00:01:07.243
Written as columns

23
00:01:07.243 --> 00:01:10.572
it is the column space of A transpose

24
00:01:10.572 --> 00:01:12.450
a subspace of R four.

25
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.835
Eliminate.

26
00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:17.148
Every row of R is a combination

27
00:01:17.251 --> 00:01:21.703
of rows of A, and every step can be undone

28
00:01:21.806 --> 00:01:24.187
so elimination does not

29
00:01:24.291 --> 00:01:26.465
change the row space.

30
00:01:26.465 --> 00:01:30.812
Its basis is the two nonzero rows: r one

31
00:01:30.812 --> 00:01:35.367
one, zero, two, minus one, and r two, zero

32
00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:36.920
one, one, one.

33
00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:37.954
Check

34
00:01:37.954 --> 00:01:42.746
row one of A is one r one plus one r two.

35
00:01:42.746 --> 00:01:47.765
Row two is two r one plus three r two: two

36
00:01:47.765 --> 00:01:49.932
three, seven, one.

37
00:01:49.932 --> 00:01:54.381
Row three is one r one plus two r two.

38
00:01:54.381 --> 00:01:58.830
So the row space has dimension r, two.

39
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.020
Careful: elimination keeps the row

40
00:02:02.104 --> 00:02:04.108
space and the nullspace

41
00:02:04.108 --> 00:02:06.280
but not the column space.

42
00:02:06.364 --> 00:02:08.786
The columns of R are not even

43
00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:10.624
in the drone's plane

44
00:02:10.624 --> 00:02:13.380
one minus zero plus zero is one.

45
00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.390
The left nullspace is every y in R three with

46
00:02:19.511 --> 00:02:23.855
y transpose A equal to the zero row

47
00:02:23.855 --> 00:02:27.475
y multiplies A from the left.

48
00:02:27.475 --> 00:02:32.423
For the drone, y is one, minus one, one

49
00:02:32.423 --> 00:02:35.560
east minus north plus up.

50
00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:40.874
Column by column: one minus two plus one

51
00:02:40.874 --> 00:02:44.022
one minus three plus two

52
00:02:44.022 --> 00:02:47.534
three minus seven plus four

53
00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:50.561
zero minus one plus one.

54
00:02:50.561 --> 00:02:53.830
All zero: the rows cancel.

55
00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.840
This is the reachability test again.

56
00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.568
If A x equals b

57
00:02:59.568 --> 00:03:03.940
then y transpose b equals y transpose A x

58
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.465
which is zero.

59
00:03:05.567 --> 00:03:08.617
Here that is b one minus b two

60
00:03:08.718 --> 00:03:11.260
plus b three equals zero.

61
00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.267
Where to find y

62
00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:16.478
reduce A next to the identity

63
00:03:16.478 --> 00:03:19.689
and the right block becomes E

64
00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:21.864
with E A equal to R.

65
00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:24.453
The row of E in front of

66
00:03:24.556 --> 00:03:26.939
the zero row of R is y.

67
00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:30.356
So the left nullspace is a line

68
00:03:30.356 --> 00:03:34.810
dimension m minus r, three minus two, one.

69
00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.396
Each vector in a basis of the left

70
00:03:38.485 --> 00:03:41.491
nullspace gives one condition that

71
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.702
a reachable b must pass.

72
00:03:43.702 --> 00:03:47.150
A five by three matrix has rank three.

73
00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.007
How many conditions?

74
00:03:49.007 --> 00:03:50.510
Pause and count.

75
00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.580
m minus r: five minus three, two conditions.

76
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.046
All four dimensions come from m, n and r.

77
00:04:04.046 --> 00:04:08.097
Row space r, nullspace n minus r

78
00:04:08.097 --> 00:04:12.745
these two live in R n and add up to n.

79
00:04:12.745 --> 00:04:17.749
Column space r, left nullspace m minus r

80
00:04:17.749 --> 00:04:21.920
these live in R m and add up to m.

81
00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.911
The spaces of the drone are too big to draw

82
00:04:25.911 --> 00:04:29.304
so take the robot matrix of lecture six

83
00:04:29.304 --> 00:04:32.366
one, two, three, six, with rank one.

84
00:04:32.366 --> 00:04:35.676
Here all four spaces live in the plane.

85
00:04:35.676 --> 00:04:37.248
On the input side

86
00:04:37.248 --> 00:04:40.890
the row space is the line through one, two

87
00:04:40.972 --> 00:04:43.124
and the nullspace the line

88
00:04:43.207 --> 00:04:45.110
through minus two, one.

89
00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.095
On the output side

90
00:04:47.095 --> 00:04:50.708
the column space is the line through one

91
00:04:50.708 --> 00:04:53.634
three, and the left nullspace the

92
00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.301
line through three, minus one

93
00:04:56.301 --> 00:05:00.000
three times row one minus row two is zero.

94
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.700
Four lines, dimensions one, one, one, one.

95
00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.970
The surprise is that the row space and the column

96
00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.010
space always have the same dimension

97
00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.650
both equal the number of pivots.

98
00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.970
Row rank equals column rank.

99
00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:16.872
So

100
00:05:16.872 --> 00:05:21.701
the row space and the nullspace live in R n

101
00:05:21.808 --> 00:05:24.384
the column space and the

102
00:05:24.491 --> 00:05:26.852
left nullspace in R m.

103
00:05:26.852 --> 00:05:30.608
Their dimensions are r, n minus r

104
00:05:30.608 --> 00:05:35.115
r and m minus r: for the drone, two, two

105
00:05:35.115 --> 00:05:36.510
two and one.

106
00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.224
Each vector y in the left

107
00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:41.305
nullspace is a check

108
00:05:41.305 --> 00:05:43.858
y transpose b equals zero

109
00:05:43.858 --> 00:05:47.168
that a reachable target must pass.

110
00:05:47.168 --> 00:05:48.208
Your turn

111
00:05:48.303 --> 00:05:51.518
give the four dimensions for a six

112
00:05:51.612 --> 00:05:54.260
by four matrix of rank four.
